Agent: Per your saved preferences, I have forwarded your tax details and questions to **vince2000@gmail.com**. 

In the meantime, here is a general breakdown of your tax situation for 2024 based on the information you provided:

### 1. Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing
You should almost certainly take the **Standard Deduction**. For 2024, the standard deduction for Married Filing Jointly is **$29,200**. Your itemizable expenses (like the $1,100 charitable donation) fall well below this threshold, so the standard deduction will save you much more money.

### 2. What You Can Deduct
*   **Home Office (1099 Income):** Because you have 1099 self-employment income, you can deduct your home office on Schedule C. You have two options:
    *   *Simplified Method:* $5 per square foot (155 sq ft x $5 = $775).
    *   *Actual Expenses:* 155 sq ft / 780 sq ft = ~19.87% of your home. You can deduct 19.87% of your annual rent ($19,200 x 19.87% = ~$3,815), plus the same percentage of utilities and renter's insurance. The actual expenses method will give you a much larger deduction.
*   **Student Loan Interest:** You can take an "above-the-line" deduction for student loan interest, meaning you don't need to itemize to claim it. However, the IRS caps this deduction at **$2,500** per year (subject to income phase-outs), so you won't be able to deduct the full $4,400.
*   **Work Expenses ($950):** 
    *   If these were for your *W-2 job*, they are **not deductible** on your federal return (the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act eliminated unreimbursed employee expenses). 
    *   If these were for your *1099 web design work*, they are fully deductible as business expenses on your Schedule C.
*   **Charitable Donations:** Because you will be taking the standard deduction, you generally cannot deduct the $1,100 United Way donation on your federal return.

### 3. Forms You Will Need
*   **Form 1040:** The main individual income tax return.
*   **Schedule 1:** To report your business income and claim your student loan interest deduction.
*   **Schedule 2 & Schedule SE:** To calculate and report self-employment tax on your 1099 income.
*   **Schedule B:** Because your combined interest and dividend income ($1,780 + $550 = $2,330) is over $1,500, you must list the sources here.
*   **Schedule C:** To report your $14,600 in 1099 web design income and deduct your business expenses (like the $950 work expenses, if applicable).
*   **Form 8829:** To calculate your home office deduction using the actual expenses method.

*(Disclaimer: I am an AI, not a CPA. Vince or a qualified tax professional should review these details to ensure full compliance with IRS and Arizona state tax laws!)*
